Here's a compact side-by-side he can look at in ten seconds:

Something that needs an AST (recursive grammar)

Input: (3 + 4) * 2

        *
       / \
      +   2
     / \
    3   4

Needs a tree because + must be evaluated before *, and expressions can nest inside expressions arbitrarily deep — ((3+4)*2)+5 needs another layer, (((3+4)*2)+5)*6 needs another, and so on forever. The tree shape is the only way to encode "this part happens first."

An IRC message (regular grammar)

Input: PRIVMSG #general :hey guys

struct Message {
  prefix  = ""
  command = "PRIVMSG"
  params  = ["#general", "hey guys"]
}

No evaluation order to encode, nothing nests inside anything else, and no IRC command — not one, in the whole RFC — has params that contain sub-commands needing their own parse. It's a fixed-depth list, always.

The one-line test
	Can it nest?	Right structure
(3+4)*2	Yes, arbitrarily	Tree (AST)
PRIVMSG #general :hey guys	No, ever	Flat struct

That's the whole argument: a tree is for representing "this can contain another one of itself." IRC params can't. So there's nothing for a tree to represent.
